The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight, which is white light, consists of different colors with varying wavelengths. When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules and small particles, and shorter wavelengths (like blue and violet) scatter more efficiently than longer wavelengths. This scattering causes the blue light to be spread out in all directions, making the sky appear blue to our eyes. [end of text]


